# checks/entry-points.allow — the reviewed list of every environment or argument read in the crates the
# shipped bridge links from outside the workspace.
#
# The ONLY list checks/entry-points.sh compares against (NFDR QC-30, half 2). The script finds the reads;
# this file says which are accepted and what each changes. See the script's header for what it reads and
# what it does not hold.
#
# One line per read:   <crate> | <name> | <what the read changes in the shipped process>
#   <crate>  the crate's name, no version: a read in either of two resolved versions is one line.
#   <name>   one of env::var, env::var_os, env::vars, env::vars_os, env::args, env::args_os, written in full.
#   text     non-empty; it may itself contain `|`. It says what the read changes in the process that ships,
#            and says so plainly when the occurrence is documentation or test code that never runs there.
# Blank lines and lines whose first non-blank character is `#` are ignored. Fields are trimmed.
#
# A line is MALFORMED, and fails the run naming its number, when it lacks its crate, its name or its text,
# names something other than the six, or repeats the crate and name of an earlier line. A malformed line
# takes no part in the comparison. The list must hold at least one line.
#
# The comparison fails in both directions: a read with no line here is unlisted, and a line whose read the
# scan no longer finds is stale. A change that moves Cargo.lock edits this file in the same commit
# (NFDR QC-30, QC-26), so the list cannot go stale unnoticed.
#
# What review of THIS file checks, past what the script can (NFDR QC-30, CP-405):
#   - that each text is true: read the actual occurrence at the version the lock resolves, and say what it
#     changes, including "nothing" for a doc comment, a README or a test helper;
#   - that no read of the shipped process is accepted without that being said in its text.
# The script never judges a text beyond its being non-empty.

git2 | env::var | src/build.rs only: a doc-comment example (`env::var("HOME")` at `:L31`, inside a `no_run` doctest for `RemoteCallbacks`); documentation, so the shipped process reads nothing. libgit2's own C reads of HOME and the XDG variables are not an `env::` name and are not held here (see the change design's "Reads outside the folder")
libz-sys | env::var | zng/cc.rs only: the zlib-ng source build's `CARGO_CFG_TARGET_OS`, `CARGO_CFG_TARGET_ARCH`, `CARGO_CFG_TARGET_FEATURE` and `TARGET` reads, which `build.rs` compiles only under the `zlib-ng-no-cmake-experimental-community-maintained` feature (`build.rs:L175-L186`); the lock resolves only `libc` for libz-sys, so the file is not compiled and the shipped process reads nothing
libz-sys | env::var_os | zng/cc.rs only: `OUT_DIR` at `:L105`, read by the same zlib-ng source build, behind the same feature the lock does not resolve; a build-script input and not part of the shipped process
quote | env::var_os | README.md only: a build.rs usage example in the documentation (`env::var_os("OUT_DIR")`); not compiled, so the shipped process reads nothing
syn | env::args | src/file.rs only: a doc comment's example program (`env::args()`); documentation, so the shipped process reads nothing
syn | env::var | tests/repo/mod.rs only: syn's own test helper reads ABORT_AFTER_FAILURE and RUST_MIN_STACK; a test, not part of the library, so the shipped process reads nothing
